This assertion is checking if "New_arrListLastIndexOfX" is less than or equal to "Old_arrListLastIndexOfX", with a message providing details about the error that should occur.--------------------The following code will convert the comment to NUnit Assert statements:

```cs

using System;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;
using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;
using PexAPIWrapper;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveContract_Assert([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assume.That(arrList.IndexOf(x) >= 0); // @@@ the index of x is greater than or equal to 0

            // Old and new value definition
            int oldCount = arrList.Count;
            arrList.Remove(x);
            int newCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.That(newCount, Is.LessThanOrEqualTo(oldCount)); // @@@ new count is less than or equal to old count
        }
    }
}
```

The comment "@@@ the index of x is greater than or equal to 0" is equivalent to Assuming arrList.IndexOf(x) >= 0 and is now converted into an assertion condition for new count to be less than or equal to old count. Remember to replace this dummy comment with actual comments from your codebase.--------------------Using regular expressions in C# to print out the Debug.Assert statements:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

namespace ConsoleApp1
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var pattern = @"\/\/\s*@@@\s*(.*)";  // This will look for lines starting with "@@@" 
            string text =
@"...your csharp code here...";   // Input your csharp code here
            
            Regex rgx = new Regex(pattern, RegexOptions.Multiline);
            MatchCollection matches = rgx.Matches(text);

            foreach (Match m in matches)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(m.Groups[1].Value.Trim() + "\n"); // Outputs the assert statement
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code will print all the assert statements which are included in comments using a regular expression.

However, note that this code will only output the comments associated with Debug.Assert statements. It may need tweaking if you want to target specific Debug.Assert conditions or modify Debug.Assert in some way, as it cannot extract or output inline code to Debug.Assert.

If you need to parse inline code into Debug.Assert statements, you might need to use a Python/Java/C# combination, as C# does not inherently support this type of inline code extraction. The same goes for trying to generate actual Debug.Assert in code. The best practice in this case is probably to split different conditions into separate Debug.Assert lines, so that you can more easily debug and fix them one by one.--------------------Your output should list all "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" in the form of corresponding csharp code. Please note that your program might not work correctly. Debugging conditions based on the asserts is not typically part of unit tests. They are typically used to stop a program if it violates an invariant condition.

